 * facts to decide it are, under the lock. What this class adds is the last step, below:
 * which status code each of those refusals is worth.
 *
 * <p><b>The refusal handlers sit on the controller rather than in a {@code
 * @ControllerAdvice}, and that is a decision about precedence rather than about tidiness.</b>
 * Spring searches the controller's own class for a handler and only then walks the advice
 * beans, so a handler here is reached by the structure of the lookup. An advice would be
 * reached by <em>winning a sort</em> against {@code ProblemDocumentAdvice}, which declares a
 * handler for {@code Exception} and would otherwise answer every refusal below as a {@code
